  "hints": [
    "An order exists exactly when the directed graph has no cycle.",
    "Kahn's algorithm: repeatedly take a task with no unfinished prerequisites (in-degree 0).",
    "Count how many tasks you remove. If it is less than n, the remaining tasks are stuck in a cycle."
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function canSchedule(n, rules) {\n  const next = Array.from({ length: n }, () => [])\n  const indegree = new Array(n).fill(0)\n  for (const [a, b] of rules) {\n    next[a].push(b)\n    indegree[b]++\n  }\n  const ready = []\n  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indegree[i] === 0) ready.push(i)\n  let done = 0\n  while (ready.length) {\n    const task = ready.pop()\n    done++\n    for (const after of next[task]) {\n      if (--indegree[after] === 0) ready.push(after)\n    }\n  }\n  return done === n\n}\n",
    "py": "def can_schedule(n, rules):\n    following = [[] for _ in range(n)]\n    indegree = [0] * n\n    for a, b in rules:\n        following[a].append(b)\n        indegree[b] += 1\n    ready = [i for i in range(n) if indegree[i] == 0]\n    done = 0\n    while ready:\n        task = ready.pop()\n        done += 1\n        for after in following[task]:\n            indegree[after] -= 1\n            if indegree[after] == 0:\n                ready.append(after)\n    return done == n\n",
    "explanation": "Kahn's algorithm only releases a task once all of its prerequisites are done. Tasks on a cycle always keep at least one unfinished prerequisite, so they are never released, and the count falls short of n. Duplicate rules are handled because each copy adds and removes one in-degree."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n + r)",
    "space": "O(n + r)",
    "notes": "r is the number of rules. A DFS with three colours (unvisited, in progress, done) is the recursive alternative."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Return a valid order as well. Which order is produced if you always pick the smallest ready task?",
    "How would a build system use this to run independent tasks in parallel?"
  ]
